WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.160
Hi. This is Matt Baker. Today we’re once again&nbsp;
going to take a look at the British royal family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:12.160
and check in on what each member’s titles&nbsp;
and ranks are as of 2026. Along the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.280
I will explain to you what those&nbsp;
titles mean, how they work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.400
and what changes we can expect to see in&nbsp;
the future, once William becomes king.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.560
Let’s start with the word Monarch. In the UK (and&nbsp;
all other Commonwealth realms), the monarch is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:32.320
the person who serves as the ceremonial head&nbsp;
of state. There can only ever be one monarch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:38.160
at any given time and that person almost always&nbsp;
holds the position for life, after which it gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.480
passed on to his or her most senior descendant.&nbsp;
If the monarch is a male (such as Charles III),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.400
he is called a King; whereas if the monarch is a&nbsp;
female (like the former monarch, Elizabeth II),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.640
she is called a Queen. However, it gets a bit&nbsp;
complicated because the wife of a king is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:03.200
called a queen (such as in the case of Queen&nbsp;
Camilla). But in her case, Camilla is just a Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.200
Consort (meaning that she’s the wife a monarch&nbsp;
but not a monarch herself). In contrast, when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:17.280
came to Queen Elizabeth II, she was the monarch&nbsp;
(and therefore a Queen Regnant). Queen Regnants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:24.000
outrank their husbands, and in order to make this&nbsp;
clear, their husbands are not called kings or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.240
king-consorts because, generally-speaking, a king&nbsp;
is understood to be a higher rank than a queen.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:36.480
Okay, next up are the terms Prince (for&nbsp;
males) and Princess (for females) In the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.160
to be automatically born a prince or princess,&nbsp;
one has to be either: a child of a monarch;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.000
or a grandchild of a monarch in a male-line.&nbsp;
The easiest way to explain this is to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.560
the children and grandchildren of Queen Elizabeth&nbsp;
II. She had three sons and one daughter so that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:01.120
three princes and one princess. Easy. However,&nbsp;
when it comes to her eight grandchildren, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:07.440
those born in a male-line (meaning to one of her&nbsp;
sons) have the right to be a prince or princess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.440
This means that Princess Anne’s children,&nbsp;
Peter and Zara, are not a prince or princess;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.160
but that William, Harry, Beatrice and Eugenie&nbsp;
all are. Edward’s two children, James and Louise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.040
are a unique case because while legally, they&nbsp;
have the right to be a prince or princess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.240
their parents turned down those titles on&nbsp;
their behalf before they were even born.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.840
Now, there is actually a third way to become a&nbsp;
prince or princess but before I tell you about it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:41.600
I want to show some of the wallcharts that we sell&nbsp;
over on our website UsefulCharts.com. First of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:48.400
all, there's our European Royal Family Tree - West&nbsp;
poster, which not only traces the British royals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:55.120
back to Kenneth MacAlpin and Alfred the Great but&nbsp;
also charts out the various dynasties from France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:01.653
the Holy Roman Empire, Spain, and more. We also&nbsp;
have a matching European Royal Family Tree - North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.653 --> 00:03:08.480
/ East chart which focuses on Scandinavia&nbsp;
and places like Russia and Poland; and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.960
East Asian Royal Family Tree that covers China,&nbsp;
Japan, and Korea; not to mention this one about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.080
Roman Emperors. If you're interested in learning&nbsp;
more about any of these wallcharts, you can head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:26.320
over to our website UsefulCharts.com by clicking&nbsp;
the link in the description or pinned comment.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:33.040
Today is a great day to check us out because right&nbsp;
now we're running a Buy One Get One Free sale.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.720
Okay, so the third way to become a prince or&nbsp;
princess is for a monarch to make a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.800
decree. This is actually how Charles and Anne&nbsp;
became a prince and princess at birth. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:50.320
they were both born before their mother became&nbsp;
Queen. At the time, they were the grandchildren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:57.600
of a monarch (George VI) but not in a male-line.&nbsp;
Therefore, George VI had to make a special decree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.200
stating that all of Elizabeth’s children would be&nbsp;
born a prince or princess, regardless of whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.720
or not she had yet become Queen. Something similar&nbsp;
happened with William’s three children, who were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:14.480
all born as the great-grandchildren of a monarch.&nbsp;
Before they were born, Queen Elizabeth decreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:20.160
that, because their father was in the direct line&nbsp;
to the throne, they too would be born a prince or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.520
princess. This created a bit of a controversy&nbsp;
because, when Harry’s two children were born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.960
they were not born a prince and princess.&nbsp;
However, they have since obtained those titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:36.880
because now that Charles III is king, they are&nbsp;
the grandchildren of a monarch in a male-line.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.120
Now, in the UK, there is also one special type of&nbsp;
prince known as the Prince of Wales. That title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.480
is only ever given to a male who is first in&nbsp;
line to the throne, such as the current holder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:53.360
Prince William. However, take note that&nbsp;
the title Prince of Wales is not given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:59.200
automatically. Instead, it must be given by&nbsp;
way of a special decree. So, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.640
Prince William did not become Prince of Wales&nbsp;
the moment that Queen Elizabeth died. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.760
he became Prince of Wales one day later,&nbsp;
when his father made a special statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.200
naming him as such. Likewise, Charles himself&nbsp;
did not become Prince of Wales the moment his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.680
mother became queen. In that case, the title came&nbsp;
six years later, when Charles was 9. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:28.160
there is also often a Princess of Wales. In&nbsp;
Diana’s case, she became Princess of Wales the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:33.840
moment she married Charles. However, in the case&nbsp;
of Catherine, since she was already married to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.720
William, she became Princess of Wales at the exact&nbsp;
same moment that William became Prince of Wales.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:46.320
But there’s an important thing to note about&nbsp;
females who are princesses by marriage. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:53.040
they are in fact princesses, they don’t get to use&nbsp;
the title “Princess” before their first name. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:59.200
even though everyone refers to Diana as “Princess&nbsp;
Diana” that’s not really correct. She was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:06.640
ever Diana, Princess of Wales. Likewise, Catherine&nbsp;
is not “Princess Catherine”. She is Catherine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.240
Princess of Wales. A small difference, yes,&nbsp;
but that’s the thing with royal titles. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:18.000
are very precise. In contrast, Catherine’s&nbsp;
daughter Charlotte can in fact be referred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.320
to as Princess Charlotte (because&nbsp;
she’s a princess in her own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:29.760
not my marriage). Which brings me to Meghan. Yes,&nbsp;
she is a princess. In her case, she can styled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:36.560
as Princess Henry (Henry being Harry’s legal&nbsp;
name) but never as Princess Meghan or Meghan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:42.880
Princess. The same goes for other princesses&nbsp;
by marriage, such as Edward’s wife Sophie.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:48.080
Now, like there is one special type of&nbsp;
prince, there is also one special type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:54.480
of princess and that is the Princess Royal.&nbsp;
Like the title Prince of Wales, this is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.440
a title that anyone gets automatically.&nbsp;
It is only ever given by special decree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:06.960
and always to an eldest daughter of a monarch.&nbsp;
The current holder is Princess Anne. In her case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.760
she was not given the title when her mother,&nbsp;
Queen Elizabeth became queen. This is because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:18.320
at that time, there already was a Princess&nbsp;
Royal—Elizabeth’s aunt Mary—and there can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:24.560
only ever be one Princess Royal. This is&nbsp;
also why Elizabeth was never Princess Royal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.440
even though she too was the eldest daughter&nbsp;
of a monarch. You should also take note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:35.120
the husband of a Princess Royal does not&nbsp;
become the Prince Royal. Princess Anne’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.960
current husband is however a “Sir” because&nbsp;
he was knighted by Queen Elizabeth in 2011.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.560
Okay, now closely related to the terms prince&nbsp;
and princess are the honorifics “His Royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.720
Highness” and “Her Royal Highness” (both&nbsp;
abbreviated HRH). First of all, take note,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:59.280
that these terms are not used for the king and&nbsp;
queen. Instead, they are His Majesty and Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.800
Majesty. HRH is only used for those at the rank of&nbsp;
prince or princess. So, for example, this includes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.480
William and Catherine and their children, as&nbsp;
well as Beatrice and Eugenie. Legally, Harry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:19.440
Meghan (as well as their two children) also have&nbsp;
the right to use HRH but they have agreed not to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.160
since they are no longer working as&nbsp;
senior royal family members. Likewise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:30.400
James and Louise also have the right to use HRH&nbsp;
but since they are not using Prince or Princess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:38.160
they don’t use HRH either. This is why I’ve shown&nbsp;
HRH in parenthesis for all of these people. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:45.040
one contentious issue related to this particular&nbsp;
honorific is the fact that Diana lost her HRH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:51.040
when she and Charles divorced. She did get to&nbsp;
keep the title Princess of Wales but she was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:56.800
longer referred to as “Her Royal Highness”. This&nbsp;
is supposedly something that has always bugged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.320
her son William and there are rumours that&nbsp;
once he becomes king, he will posthumously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:09.200
give Diana back her HRH. Another example&nbsp;
of someone who lost his HRH is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:16.160
Andrew. Unlike Harry, who simply doesn’t use&nbsp;
the phrase, Andrew was legally stripped of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:22.400
in addition to losing his title of Prince (due to&nbsp;
his alleged crimes and open connections to Jeffrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:29.840
Epstein). His ex-wife Sarah lost her HRH at the&nbsp;
same time and ceased to be a princess back when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:36.240
they divorced. They are now referred to simply&nbsp;
as Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor and Sarah Ferguson.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.040
Okay, so far, everything that I’ve talked&nbsp;
about has been related to titles that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:47.280
royal family members can hold, such as King,&nbsp;
Queen, Prince, and Princess. I’m now going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:53.200
move on and talk about Peerage Titles, which are&nbsp;
a little bit different, because they can be held,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.720
not only by royal family members but also&nbsp;
by members of the nobility (which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.720 --> 00:10:05.280
social class just below that of royalty). In&nbsp;
the UK, there are five ranks in the peerage:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:13.360
Duke, Marquess, Earl, Viscount, and Baron&nbsp;
(with the female equivalents being Duchess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:22.800
Marchioness, Countess, Viscountess, and Baroness.&nbsp;
Below these do come Baronets, Knights / Dames,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:28.720
and the Landed Gentry but take note that these&nbsp;
three ranks are not included in the peerage.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:33.840
Now, originally, all of these titles were&nbsp;
connected to actual leadership roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:39.680
with dukes controlling large areas called&nbsp;
duchies, marquesses controlling border areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:45.600
called marches, earls and viscounts controlling&nbsp;
counties, and so on. But these days, the titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:53.120
are almost entirely ceremonial. I say “almost”&nbsp;
because having a peerage does make you eligible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:11:00.080
to serve in the House of Lords, which is a part&nbsp;
of the UK Parliament. However, I also say “almost"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:06.240
because there are in fact still two duchies&nbsp;
remaining in the UK that are entirely owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:13.840
by and controlled by specific dukes. These are&nbsp;
the Duchy of Lancaster and Duchy of Cornwall. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:19.680
who are dukes of these two areas? Well, the&nbsp;
Duke of Lancaster is none other than Charles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:27.840
III. This is because, way back in 1399, the Duke&nbsp;
of Lancaster became king so ever since then, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.760
particular duchy has been controlled by the crown&nbsp;
(although it has since become much, much smaller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:38.720
because over the years, parts of it were sold&nbsp;
off for various reasons). As for the Duchy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:46.080
Cornwall, well, ever since 1337, the duke there&nbsp;
has always been whoever the monarch’s eldest son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:53.440
is—so nowadays, it’s Prince William. If a monarch&nbsp;
doesn’t have any sons, it’s held by the crown.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:59.280
Unlike “Prince of Wales”, the title “Duke&nbsp;
of Cornwall” is automatically inherited. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:05.280
as soon as Elizabeth II became Queen, Charles&nbsp;
became Duke of Cornwall. And as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:11.600
Charles III became King, William &amp; Catherine&nbsp;
became the Duke and Duchess of Cornwall. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:17.120
long before England and Scotland merged to become&nbsp;
Great Britain, Scotland had a similar title (the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.080
Duke of [ROTH-see] Rothesay) that was always&nbsp;
inherited by the Scottish monarch’s eldest son&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:27.120
(although it was never associated with a plot of&nbsp;
land). Since England and Scotland are now part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:33.920
of a single kingdom, whoever the Duke of Cornwall&nbsp;
is, is also always the Duke of Rothesay (However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:39.920
they only ever use that title when in Scotland).&nbsp;
Now, prior to becoming Duke &amp; Duchess of Cornwall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:45.280
and Duke &amp; Duchess of Rothesay, William &amp;&nbsp;
Catherine were already the Duke &amp; Duchess of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:49.920
Cambridge (because they had been given those&nbsp;
titles on their wedding day). So nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:56.400
they are Duke &amp; Duchess three times over. However,&nbsp;
keep in mind that royals tend to use only their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:01.760
highest titles so in most cases, William is&nbsp;
simply referred to as: His Royal Highness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:06.960
the Prince of Wales; and Catherine: Her&nbsp;
Royal Highness, the Princess of Wales.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:11.760
Now, when it comes to their three children, they&nbsp;
don’t hold any extra titles beyond simply being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:17.040
Prince or Princess, at least not yet. Thus,&nbsp;
for example, the eldest is simply called:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:23.520
His Royal Highness Prince George of Wales&nbsp;
(although, take note that, at birth, he and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:29.520
siblings were called “of Cambridge” because, prior&nbsp;
to the death of Queen Elizabeth, Duke of Cambridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:36.000
was their father’s highest title). So what happens&nbsp;
when William becomes king (most likely as William&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:42.480
V)? Well, at that point, Prince George would&nbsp;
immediately become Duke of Cornwall and Duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:47.920
of Rothesay, even if he is still a minor; and&nbsp;
the other two would immediately become Princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:53.760
Charlotte of the United Kingdom and Prince Louis&nbsp;
of the United Kingdom. As for when George would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:59.040
become Prince of Wales, well, that would be up&nbsp;
to William. It could be straight away or after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:05.440
several years. During William’s reign, Princess&nbsp;
Charlotte could eventually be made Princess Royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:12.400
but that would have to occur sometime after the&nbsp;
death of Princess Anne. As for Prince Louis, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:18.000
he’ll likely be given a special dukedom on his&nbsp;
wedding day (if he gets married). Traditionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:23.840
a king’s second son is given the title Duke of&nbsp;
York, so long as it is available. Technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:29.280
it’s still held by Andrew (a topic I’ll get&nbsp;
to in a second) but even if it isn’t by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:34.240
time Louis gets married, William might&nbsp;
choose to go with something else instead.

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:40.720
Staying on the topic of dukedoms, Harry &amp; Meghan&nbsp;
were given the titles Duke &amp; Duchess of Sussex on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:45.200
their wedding day and even though there’s since&nbsp;
been a rift between them and the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:52.640
family, they do still hold and use those titles.&nbsp;
It’s just the HRH that they no longer use. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:59.680
the correct way to refer to them is: Prince Harry,&nbsp;
the Duke of Sussex; and Meghan, the Duchess of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:06.080
Sussex. You should also take note that the title&nbsp;
Duke of Sussex is hereditary and therefore it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:11.440
likely that someday, upon Prince Harry’s&nbsp;
death, the title will go to his son Archie.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:16.400
Let me now return to the titles Duke &amp;&nbsp;
Duchess of York, which until last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:20.720
were still being used by Andrew &amp; Sarah,&nbsp;
having been given to them on their wedding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:25.840
day. I did a whole other video on this topic&nbsp;
earlier this year but the long and short of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:32.320
it is that Charles (as monarch) has the power&nbsp;
to legally un-prince a prince as well as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:40.080
take away their right to use HRH but he does not&nbsp;
have the power to un-duke a duke (or any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:46.800
peerage). That can only be done through an Act of&nbsp;
Parliament. But he did find sort of a work around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:52.560
He was able to instruct the person in charge of&nbsp;
maintaining the Roll of the Peerage to simply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:58.560
remove Andrew’s name from the list (effectively&nbsp;
prohibiting Andrew from using the title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:03.840
even though he does still technically hold&nbsp;
it). Another important thing to note is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:09.920
Andrew’s “de-princing” in no way impacted&nbsp;
the titles held by his two daughters. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:15.840
they’re both still princesses and can use&nbsp;
HRH—although they have no extra titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:21.360
beyond those and their husbands and children&nbsp;
have none. Unofficially, Beatrice’s husband&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:28.960
is an Italian count but since Italy no longer&nbsp;
has a monarchy, this is a courtesy title only.

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:34.240
Far more active in the royal family is&nbsp;
the king’s youngest brother Prince Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.240 --> 00:16:40.480
and his wife Sophie. They currently hold the&nbsp;
titles Duke and Duchess of Edinburgh. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:47.200
for a long time were known by the lesser titles&nbsp;
of Earl and Countess of Wessex (as these were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:53.680
titles they received on their wedding day, back&nbsp;
in 1999). Remember, an Earl is one step down from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.680 --> 00:16:59.360
a Duke. The reason for this was that, at the&nbsp;
time, a different person held the title Duke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:04.800
of Edinburgh—his father Prince Philip—and&nbsp;
Prince Philip wanted Edward to eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:11.040
get that particular title. But this couldn’t&nbsp;
happen until after both he and Queen Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:17.360
had died. You see, Philip was given the title as&nbsp;
a hereditary one, which meant that when he died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:23.600
it automatically passed to his eldest son Charles&nbsp;
(who at the time was still a prince). However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:30.080
when the Queen died and Charles ascended to&nbsp;
the throne, the title became extinct because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:36.320
aside from holding the Duchy of Lancaster,&nbsp;
a King cannot also be a Duke. This freed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:42.560
the title to finally be given to Edward,&nbsp;
which it was in 2023. This time though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:49.760
it was given as a non-hereditary peerage and&nbsp;
thus, it will not eventually pass to his son.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:56.080
Currently, James uses the courtesy title Earl&nbsp;
of Wessex, because, within the British nobility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.080 --> 00:18:02.240
sons are permitted to use one of their father’s&nbsp;
lesser titles prior to actually inheriting it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:09.520
His sister Louise uses the title “Lady” as that&nbsp;
is the customary prefix used by the daughters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:15.120
of dukes and earls but because she has no&nbsp;
titles of her own, she simply goes by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:20.400
surname Mountbatten-Windsor (Mountbatten&nbsp;
being the house name of her grandfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:25.520
Prince Philip and Windsor being the house&nbsp;
name of her grandmother Queen Elizabeth).

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:30.800
Now, there are two more dukedoms connected&nbsp;
to the royal family and to explain those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:39.440
we have to go back to the time of George&nbsp;
V. He had four sons who lived to adulthood:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:47.760
Edward became Prince of Wales, Albert became&nbsp;
Duke of York, Henry became Duke of Gloucester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:54.320
and George became Duke of Kent. Edward went&nbsp;
on to become king but then abdicated and thus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.320 --> 00:19:01.200
Albert went on to become king as well, choosing&nbsp;
to reign as George VI, eventually being succeeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:06.720
by his eldest daughter Elizabeth. As for&nbsp;
Henry and George, well, after they died,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:13.840
their dukedoms passed to their eldest surviving&nbsp;
sons. So the current holders today are the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:20.160
Queen’s cousins Prince Richard (who’s the Duke&nbsp;
of Gloucester) and Prince Edward (the Duke of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:26.480
Kent). Edward’s younger brother Michael does&nbsp;
not have a peerage so he’s simply called “of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:33.600
Kent” and their sister, well, she’s called Lady&nbsp;
Ogilvy because she was married to a Sir Ogilvy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:40.960
(who held the rank of knight). All four of these&nbsp;
individuals are prince or princess and can use HRH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:46.880
because they are all male-line grandchildren&nbsp;
of a previous monarch. Their children however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:52.320
are not prince or princess, although the&nbsp;
dukedoms will continue to be passed on.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:59.120
Which brings me to the term “working royal”. This&nbsp;
is not an official title but rather a role that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:04.960
certain members of the royal family have. As you&nbsp;
can see, the royal family at this point is quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:12.160
large. However, only eleven of them are what we&nbsp;
call “working royals” (meaning that they attend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.160 --> 00:20:18.560
special events and represent the royal family&nbsp;
while doing so). These are the King and Queen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:24.960
the Prince and Princess of Wales, the Duke&nbsp;
and Duchess of Edinburgh, the Princess Royal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:30.560
the Duke and Duchess of Gloucester,&nbsp;
the Duke of Kent, and the Lady Ogilvy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:36.080
(although take note that these last two have&nbsp;
cut back on their duties considerably in recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:43.200
years due to their age and health—Prince&nbsp;
Edward being 91 and Lady Ogilvy 89).

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:51.120
Another role that only some royal family members&nbsp;
have is that of Counsellor of State (which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:56.720
that they are allowed to legally represent&nbsp;
the monarch, if the monarch is unavailable).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:03.200
Usually it’s the spouse of the monarch and then&nbsp;
the four highest ranking adults in the line of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:11.120
succession that hold that privilege but due to a&nbsp;
special decree made by Charles III, Prince Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:19.760
and Princess Anne were made Counsellor of State&nbsp;
also so that Harry and Andrew could be avoided.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:25.120
Now, there’s one more thing that I need address&nbsp;
and that is the line of succession. The UK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:32.080
follows a system called Primogeniture (which is&nbsp;
Latin for first birth). This means that the line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.080 --> 00:21:39.680
always proceeds downward along the family tree,&nbsp;
from firstborn to firstborn, and only to the side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:46.480
to siblings when there are no more direct heirs&nbsp;
available. So, first in line is Prince William,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:53.520
followed by his three children—in birth order.&nbsp;
Take note that, for those born after 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.520 --> 00:22:00.400
it doesn’t matter if a child is male or female.&nbsp;
So this is why Charlotte comes before Louis. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:07.920
Louis comes Harry and his two children and then&nbsp;
Andrew and his daughters &amp; grandchildren. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:13.120
Harry and Andrew are still in the line of&nbsp;
succession. This is because yet another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:19.680
thing a monarch cannot do is remove someone&nbsp;
the line of succession. So, even though Harry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:26.880
is not a working royal and even though Andrew&nbsp;
is under police investigation, they are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:33.120
in the line of succession. The only thing that&nbsp;
can get someone removed is if that person dies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:40.640
ceases to be a Protestant Christian, or if the&nbsp;
parliaments of all 15 commonwealth realms pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.640 --> 00:22:47.600
legislation to remove that person. So, even if&nbsp;
Andrew were put in jail, he’d still be eligible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:53.600
to become king. Practically speaking though, the&nbsp;
chances of that ever happening is extremely low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:58.640
which is why there hasn’t been a rush for&nbsp;
the parliaments to discuss the matter.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:04.080
New to the line of succession this year&nbsp;
is Eugenie’s third child, Adelaide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:09.760
who was born in August. Now, another thing I&nbsp;
want to point out is that even though Anne was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:16.960
born before Andrew &amp; Edward, she comes after them&nbsp;
because the recent switch from male-preference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:23.680
primogeniture to absolute primogeniture only&nbsp;
applies to those born after that change was made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:31.440
which was in 2011. So next comes Edward and&nbsp;
his two children, then Anne, her children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:36.400
and grandchildren. Also new to the tree&nbsp;
this year is Peter's new wife Harriet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:43.680
who he married in June. But remember, neither&nbsp;
Peter nor his sister Zara hold any royal titles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:49.280
as they were not grandchildren in a male-line.&nbsp;
However, of all the people on this tree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:55.840
it is actually Zara and her husband Mike who&nbsp;
are the closest (friendship-wise) to William,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:55.840 --> 00:24:01.920
the future king (in addition to both being very&nbsp;
popular with the public). It is thus possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:07.600
that we might see them receive titles some&nbsp;
day although it is also likely that William&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.600 --> 00:24:13.200
might avoid giving out titles in an effort to&nbsp;
keep the royal family as small as possible.

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:20.400
Anyhow, after Princess Anne's family, comes&nbsp;
the descendants of the Queen’s sister Margaret,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:27.200
followed by the descendants of these four older&nbsp;
royals that I pointed out earlier. If you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:33.680
see exactly who all of those descendents are, you&nbsp;
can check out this video that I made last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:39.520
which goes through the first 60 or so people&nbsp;
in the line of succession in greater detail.

00:24:39.520 --> 00:24:46.000
Okay, so that was my annual check in on the&nbsp;
British royal family and their various titles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:48.480
ranks, and roles. Let me know in the comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:52.000
what changes you think we're&nbsp;
likely to see in the future.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:57.760
And don't forget to check out our&nbsp;
wallcharts over at UsefulCharts.com.

00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:13.840
Thanks for watching.

